  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   Minimize J(a) = 2*n*max(convolve(a,a)) / (sum(a))^2\n\n   The key lies in Gaussian patterns and their interactions. We leverage sequences optimized in previous runs and test variations that were empirically demonstrated to have favorable smoothing properties. This is less risky than variations in dimensionality, which require heavier investment and local minima are more likely stable. Our focus is on finding structures, not just values.\n\n2. Mathematical Description:\n   We work entirely on the simplex (sum(a)=1, a_i\u22650). Let F = convolve(a,a) (full mode), then J(a) = 2*n * max(F) / (sum(a))^2, but with the simplex fix, J(a) = 2*n * max(F) (where F is derived from a).\n   Our algorithm's candidate sequences will specifically include:\n    - The provided parent sequence (if any)\n    - Variations of the parent sequence by expanding/halving its length\n    - Standard candidates: uniform, Gaussian, Sine/Cosine patterns\n    - Empirically validated candidates from previous runs (especially those achieving scores close to 1.5027 target)\n\n3. Implementation Plan:\n   The exact time budget is 1500 seconds. We break the time allocation across phases:\n\n   Phase 1: Initial expansion (400s). Create candidate sequences of lengths around the parent length (or imply 384 if parent not provided) via linear interpolation and uniform perturbations. This provides a grounding by diversity.\n\n   Phase 2: Validation via Evaluation-function checks (300s). For every candidate generated in previous stages, ensure we can accurately call evaluate_sequence and trust its output. This involves explicitly bounding all values and possibly reformulating the sequence.\n\n   Phase 3: Refinement via simplex projection (300s). Use projected optimization methods (like coordinate descent) on the best-structured candidates. This allows us to maximize further without dimension jitter.\n\n   Phase 4: Saving checkpoints (remaining time). The minimal J total will guide our save points. Ensure we check for minimal J at regular intervals during optimization.\n\n4. Vectors and candidate generation:\n   - Start with the parent sequence (if available) or the best previous candidate\n   - Generate sequences of lengths:\n        parent_length \u00b1 factor (use 2^10 samples means to consider different variations)\n   - Normalization is automatic by evaluate_sequence, so we set sum=1.0\n\n5. Methodology for J calculation:\n   The J value is calculated precisely by evaluate_sequence. We compare entire neighborhoods only when we have clear performance indicators.\n\n6. Time Budgeting and resource allocation:\n   - Monitor the execution clock strictly\n   - 400s for expansion: we test at least 20 candidate sequences\n   - 300s for validation: every candidate in the expansion phase uses evaluate_sequence for verification\n   - 300s for optimization: refinement without validation (unless testing new dimension)\n   - Use short timeouts for each optimization step\n\n7. Termination Conditions:\n   - If all candidates evaluated improve J above a threshold (goal: 1.5027), terminate\n   - If time expires, return the best verified candidate found\n   - Ensure the final J calculation is done solely by evaluate_sequence for the final candidate\n\n8. Algorithm Structure:\n   Let max_time = 1500\n   start = time.time()\n\n   def propose_candidate(budget):\n        global_sequence = provided_parent (if available) or None\n        best_sequence = None\n        best_J = float('inf')\n        sequences_to_try = []\n        low = high = None\n\n        # If no global_parent available, try smallest fixed set of sequences\n        if global_sequence is None:\n            try base_pattern = None\n            sequences_to_try = [ generate_uniform(logger, eval_time), ... and Gaussian base ]\n\n        else:\n            Logger.info(f\"Parent sequence available, length={len(global_sequence)}\")\n            sequences_to_try.append(global_sequence)\n            # Check horizontal shifts\n            sequences_to_try.append(shift_sequence(global_sequence, 1))\n            sequences_to_try.append(shift_sequence(global_sequence, 0.5))\n            sequences_to_try.append(shift_sequence(global_sequence, 0.75))\n\n        # For each sequence candidate in sequences_to_try,\n        # compute J using evaluate_sequence\n\n        # First, allocate 400s for expansion:\n        expansion_sequences = []\n        for seed, logger in enumerate([5, 3, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19]):\n            try_sequence = variation_from_parent(global_sequence, seeds, dimensions)\n            new_J = evaluate_sequence(try_sequence)\n            if new_J < best_J:\n                best_J = new_J\n                best_sequence = try_sequence.copy()\n\n        # Then, use at least 300s for validation of promising sequences\n        promising_sequences = evaluate_all_at_low_time(logger, seed, time)\n\n        # Then, use the best_sequence to generate further variations and optimize\n\n        # Finally, ensure minimal J via simplex projection and coordinate descent\n\n   After compiling a set of promising sequences, the algorithm then enters a costly but directed optimization phase:\n\n        candidates = set()\n\n        while time < max_time and improvement detected:\n            use the best_current sequence to generate new variations via coordinate descent (binding extremes and smoothing function)\n            evaluate and update best_sequence\n\n        The final candidate must be subjected to the last evaluation by evaluate_sequence\n\n   This algorithm respects the no-copy requirement and prioritizes verified minimal J values.",
